  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "$\\,$ Either you do not water the plants, or they will grow.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You do not water the plants.\n\n- $B:$ The plants will grow.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A\u2228B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n\u00acA\u21d2B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $\u00acA:$ You water the plants.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, then they will grow.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, they will grow.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(\u00acP\u2228Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true.",
